title: "Secure Multiparty Communication Systems"
authors:
  - family-names: Lippok
    given-names: Finn Paul
    affiliation: RWTH Aachen University
    email: finn.lippok@rwth-aachen.de
repository-code: "https://github.com/FinnPL/Secure-Multiparty-Communication-Systems"
url: "https://finnpl.github.io/Secure-Multiparty-Communication-Systems/paper/seminar-paper.pdf"
abstract: >-
  Group messaging is used at a huge scale, but upholding strong security notions as in two-party communication while accommodating offline members and group changes is an ongoing effort.
  This paper compares deployed protocols such as Sender Keys (Signal and WhatsApp) and MLS (Discord VC, RCS) with current proposed improved protocols and variants against a shared threat model and security goals.
  We also consider the practicality of deployment of these protocols and how misbehaving peers can affect groups.
  Our analysis shows shortcomings in current protocols such as Sender Keys' weak notion of PCS requiring a full rekey that costs O(n^2) ciphertexts, while Continuous Group-Key Agreement heals much more cheaply.
  Even though the worst-case linear cost of MLS-style protocols cannot be prevented, as research shows, cryptography only makes up a small part of computational cost for large groups.
  Preventing misbehaving peers from splitting groups is possible, but only at higher cost using Zero-Knowledge proofs.
  No one improved protocol wins everywhere; cost can be reduced significantly when trusting the Delivery Service for ordering is acceptable for a deployment's threat model.
keywords:
  - "secure group messaging"
  - "continuous group key agreement"
  - "Messaging Layer Security"
